course: dictionary
lesson: 8171
title: Resilient: bouncing back
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Resilient means able to bounce back after hard times, and keep going. Look at the words around it to work out the meaning. «Being resilient does not mean never being knocked down; it means getting back up, usually with help, one day at a time.» The clues are knocked down and getting back up, so resilient means recovering and carrying on.
explain: Resilience is not about never feeling bad. A resilient person still feels sad or upset after a hard time. What makes them resilient is that they recover, and they do it with help from friends, from talking things through, and with time.
explain: Resilient also describes materials. A resilient material can bend or stretch and spring back into shape without breaking, like a rubber band. In science lessons, the word is used this way. In wellbeing lessons, it is used for people. The two meanings are linked: both are about bouncing back.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Being bounce-back-able does not mean never being knocked down» sounds odd, so try «Being able to recover does not mean never being knocked down» instead. If the meaning still fits, you have understood the word.
fact: Resilient means able to bounce back after hard times, or a material that springs back into shape.
fact: «Resilient children» are children who can recover from mistakes and keep going.
fact: «A resilient material» can bend or stretch and spring back without breaking.
fact: Resilience grows with help, talking and time; it does not mean never feeling bad.
fact: Resilient is an adjective: resilient, more resilient, most resilient.
exercise: «Being resilient does not mean never being knocked down.» What does resilient mean? | Easily broken | Never sad or upset | *Able to bounce back after hard times | Loud and bold
exercise: Ella says a resilient rubber ball will snap when squashed. What is wrong with her idea? | A resilient material always snaps | Resilient means heavy and hard to lift | Rubber can never bend at all | *A resilient material bends and springs back without breaking
exercise: Which action shows resilience after a mistake? | Refusing any help from others | Hiding from anything hard | *Getting back up and trying again | Giving up at the first problem
test: What does resilience usually grow with? | Being completely alone | *Help, talking and time | Giving up quickly | Never feeling bad at all
test: Which of these is a resilient material? | *Rubber that bends and springs back | Ice that melts in the sun | A brittle plate that cracks when dropped | Wet paper that tears easily
exam: «Being resilient does not mean never being knocked down; it means getting back up, usually with help.» What does it mean to be resilient? | Staying down until the feeling passes | Being knocked down on purpose | *Getting back up after being knocked down, usually with help | Never being knocked down at all
